Title:
PhantomBuffer: Effective EDR Evasion & Stealth Injection

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post describes a Windows process injection approach (“PhantomBuffer”) aimed at reducing EDR visibility by avoiding common high-signal API patterns and memory artifacts. It focuses on stealthy payload staging/execution while minimizing suspicious indicators like RWX pages, obvious remote thread creation, and noisy Win32 injection call chains. The write-up discusses how modern EDRs detect injection via user-mode API telemetry, ETW/callbacks, and memory scanning, and how to restructure injection to blend into normal process behavior. It is primarily useful for red team operators and malware developers evaluating detection boundaries and improving tradecraft, and secondarily for blue teams to understand what to hunt for beyond classic injection primitives. The key value is in mapping detection points to specific implementation choices (allocation, permissions, thread start, and execution flow) and proposing lower-noise alternatives.

Technical Focus:
- Windows process injection tradecraft and stealth execution
- Memory allocation/protection strategies (RW → RX, avoiding RWX)
- Thread creation alternatives and execution redirection
- EDR telemetry sources (user-mode hooks, ETW, callbacks) and evasion considerations
- Payload staging, mapping, and in-memory artifact reduction

Use Cases:
- Develop and test stealthier Windows injection PoCs in lab environments
- Red team tradecraft refinement against common EDR detection logic
- Build defensive detections for “non-classic” injection patterns (behavioral + memory)
- Create threat hunting hypotheses around anomalous memory permission transitions and thread start contexts

Keywords:
Windows injection, EDR evasion, stealth injection, process hollowing, remote thread, APC, NtAllocateVirtualMemory, NtProtectVirtualMemory, NtWriteVirtualMemory, VirtualAlloc, VirtualProtect, ETW, user-mode hooks, memory scanning, RWX avoidance, RX pages, thread start address, syscall, in-memory payload